WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.334
-Welcome to the show.
-Thank you.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.234
It's great to be here.
-And now you're

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.667
in the NBC/MSNBC family.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:06.534
-I am.

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.033
-Yeah, anything I can do
for you.

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:09.767
Anything -- any advice --
anything I can help,

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.467
I can give you directions
to the commissary, whatever.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.234
-I don't even know
what the commissary is.

00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:15.701
But I do need directions.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:18.167
-Yes, I will tell you exactly
where it is and what to order.

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:19.367
-I know where
the lady's room is.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.467
That's a big progression.
-Yeah, see?

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.868
Yeah, since last -- yeah.
-Since last time.

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.434
I make it last
in the elevator bank.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:28.100
So, if I'm there, my only ask is
that you fish me out of there.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.267
-I will help you, yeah.
-It's a big building.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:30.400
-I will give you my number.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.734
I'll give whatever I can do
to help you.

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:32.701
-Thank you.
-How are you doing?

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:34.400
How are the kids?
-They're great.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.801
-How old are they now?
-My daughter is 7.

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:38.033
She wakes up with rainbows

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.501
and unicorns shooting
out of her head.

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:41.167
I don't know if anybody else has
a 7-year-old.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.334
But this is how it is.
-Yeah, that's what they do.

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:43.767
That's what little girls do.
-They do.

00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:46.067
My 4-year-old is a little bit
more challenging,

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.767
but he is also a lover.

00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:49.667
He'll be brushing my hair
when I'm 100.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:53.267
My 7-year-old has told me she
wants to run for president.

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.567
So, there's that.
-Whoa!

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.634
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I know, right?

00:00:56.634 --> 00:00:58.267
Yeah.
-That's major.

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:00.167
-I'm not announcing it here
tonight

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.567
because she's got
a lot of years.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.400
She does ask me how old you need
to be to run for president.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.133
So that's the kind of
discussions we have.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.701
-Yeah, you're like, "70."

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:09.067
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:10.734
I'm like, "very old."
-Very, very old.

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:11.834
-Very old. It's a lot.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:12.801
You have to raise
a lot of money.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:14.567
It's very stressful. Exactly.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.868
-Do they have any concept of
what Mom does

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.400
or did for a living?
-Not really.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.901
I mean, which I love they don't
have any idea that I was

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:25.567
on television and talking about
hard issues.

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.501
-Yeah.
-So, I love that.

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:30.901
I did take them
to the White House.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:32.934
About a year ago, actually, was

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:35.667
the first time I took them
to the White House. I know.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.033
-And you're like, "Yeah, I know
my way around this place."

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:40.400
-Right. I'm like, "Look, guys.
Here we are in the White House.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.000
Look, there's a big building."

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.701
And it was actually the day
that the President did

00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:47.300
the turkey pardoning,
which he does every year.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.667
-Oh, that's a great day
to bring the kids.

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:49.834
-Great day to bring them.

00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:51.701
The turkeys sleep in a hotel
the night before.

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:52.767
I don't know
if people knew that.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.501
It is true.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.501 --> 00:01:56.601
And so I took them to
the turkey pardoning.

00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:58.300
They met the President.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.133
They met the turkeys,
which was a big deal.

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:03.033
I took them --
They were huge Turkeys.

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.567
It will be a big family dinner
for those turkeys.

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:06.834
I took them to -- walked around

00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:08.000
the White House
to the briefing room.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.934
-Are you allowed to go --
You're like, "Hi, guys."

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.467
You know all the security
and so you're, like, kind of --

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:13.000
-I kind of -- Right.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.901
They let you walk around
a little bit.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.434
So, that was nice.
And they loved it.

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:19.934
And I felt great about it
because I wanted my kids

00:02:19.934 --> 00:02:21.767
to experience what I did.

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:24.534
And so, at the end of the day,
I'm walking them out

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:26.901
of the White House,
and I say to my 7-year-old,

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:29.501
"So, I mean, today
was an amazing day.

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:31.501
We'll remember it.
You met the President.

00:02:31.501 --> 00:02:32.834
It was so exciting.

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:35.300
What was your favorite thing
you'll remember?"

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.501
And she was like, "Mom, I've
never had Sprite before.

00:02:38.501 --> 00:02:39.767
It was so good."

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.934
Yeah, true.
-Yes!

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:44.267
[ Applause ]
-So, there you go.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.801
-Kids are always win.
-They are amazing.

00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:49.200
They bring it down to Earth
for you.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.367
-That's an amazing thing,
to get your first Sprite.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:52.534
It's a big deal.
-It is.

00:02:52.534 --> 00:02:54.167
-It's delicious.
-And then when I would go back

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:55.434
to the White House to work,
she would say,

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:58.267
"Mom, Mom, can you bring me home
some Sprite?

00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:01.934
Can you bring me home
some Sprite in a cup?"

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:05.200
-My kids are too young
for phones, but they get to --

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.033
you know, if they have my phone
or my wife's phone,

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.400
we'll be texting each other,
which is really cute.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.133
And I texted, like,

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:12.267
"I love you,"
or something like that.

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:15.667
And she wrote, "Sun kiss."
-Oh.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:17.300
-And I go, "That's so cute."

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.868
Like -- like, "Sun --"
-Is that a good thing?

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:21.434
-I don't know.
I go -- I go, "Great.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:24.501
I love -- kiss to you too."
She goes, "Can I?"

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:26.234
-And I go, "What?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:28.267
She was saying "Sunkist"
into the phone.

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:30.067
-Oh.
-She wanted a can of soda.

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:32.133
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:34.000
-I go, "Yeah, of course.
You can have a Sunkist.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.234
We have a Sunkist at the house."

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.567
-There you go.
-Yeah, she just wanted a drink.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:37.901
-Yes, you can have a Sunkist.

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:39.667
-Big deal tomorrow night.

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:42.100
Election night.
A lot of coverage.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.133
Are you prepared for it?
Are you ready for it?

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:45.634
-I think so.

00:03:45.634 --> 00:03:48.067
I mean, I am the new kid on the
block here for election night.

00:03:48.067 --> 00:03:50.467
-Yeah.
-So, I have lots of snacks.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:51.834
I think that's kind of
what you do

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:54.200
when you go and you want
to fit in at the lunch table.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.868
I've got almonds and chocolate.

00:03:56.868 --> 00:03:57.767
-It's an all-night thing.
-All sorts of things.

00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:58.901
-You need your energy up.

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:00.300
-And you want to share them
with your --

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.267
with your new friends
and colleagues to --

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:03.801
I want to see what they --
what they like --

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:06.467
-You're the new kid at school.
-Exactly, exactly.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:09.000
-But you've been going around
the country

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.801
and seeing
what people actually --

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.434
what their issues are
and what they care

00:04:12.434 --> 00:04:14.067
about for this election.

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.334
Have you been --
What have you been finding out?

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:17.667
What do people care about?

00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:19.534
-You know, what I've found
from working in politics

00:04:19.534 --> 00:04:21.567
for a long time
is that the way people talk

00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:23.634
about issues
and things impacting them

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:26.534
is not how they talk about it
in Washington, D.C.,

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:29.100
or even sometimes
in New York City.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.767
People talk about issues

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.234
in terms of how they impact
them personally.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:36.267
And I spent a day
walking around communities,

00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:39.734
door knocking,
following some door knockers.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:43.100
And people said, "Look, I care
about abortion rights.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.467
That's of concern to me.

00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:46.834
But I also need to pay
for childcare,

00:04:46.834 --> 00:04:48.734
and I need to be able
to put food on the table.

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.200
And that's of concern to me."

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.167
So it was a reminder to me of
people in their communities --

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:54.767
I don't know about yours,
but not on my street.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.968
They don't talk about
macroeconomic data, right?

00:04:56.968 --> 00:04:59.367
They talk about how they're
living their lives,

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:01.634
how things impact
their families and their kids.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.200
And I think that's so important
to remember

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.701
for anyone running for office.

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:05.701
-Yeah.

00:05:05.701 --> 00:05:08.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.868
Is the -- Everything I've been
seeing and reading is

00:05:12.868 --> 00:05:16.067
predicting a red wave
for tomorrow.

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:17.767
Is that true?
-Doesn't that sound so dark?

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:19.167
It's -- It's a little bit --
it's a little bit --

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:20.901
Reminds me of
"Game of Thrones" or something.

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.300
-The red wave. Yeah.
-The red wave is coming.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.901
Here's the thing.
I mean, tomorrow night

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:27.567
I'm going to sit down with this
amazing group of people

00:05:27.567 --> 00:05:28.934
who have been
doing election coverage --

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:32.033
I mean, Rachel Maddow
could make paint seem exciting.

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:34.067
-Yeah, she's great.
-And she is -- You know, she --

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.334
and Nicolle Wallace,

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.234
who I was watching
when I was in the White House

00:05:37.234 --> 00:05:38.667
because she would tell me
what was happening

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:41.167
in the Department of Justice,
even though I worked there.

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:43.033
So I get to sit with them.
We're not going to know

00:05:43.033 --> 00:05:45.000
at 6:00 p.m. when we sit down

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.534
what's going to happen
at 2:00 a.m.

00:05:47.534 --> 00:05:49.667
or what's going to happen
at 3:00 a.m. and 4:00 a.m.

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:52.100
We have to follow the story
and see what's happening.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.334
Where people are voting,
where people are turning out.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:57.434
There are going to be surprises
in the election.

00:05:57.434 --> 00:05:59.467
This is what's so exciting
to watch.

00:05:59.467 --> 00:06:03.133
The good news here, is that
turnout is in record numbers

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:05.367
across the country
in a lot of places.

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:08.534
In Georgia, it is
at record numbers already.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:09.801
-That's fantastic.

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.367
-Young people are turning out
in record numbers.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:13.767
People always downplay
the engagement

00:06:13.767 --> 00:06:15.634
and involvement
of people under 30.

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:17.567
I will tell you, those are
the kind of people

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:19.200
who are the most passionate
and engaged

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.000
on the issues that matter.
-I love that.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.267
Good. Go. Vote.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.267 --> 00:06:25.434
So, those are exciting --
those are exciting things.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:27.734
But we don't know
what's going to happen.

00:06:27.734 --> 00:06:29.934
I think it's our responsibility
tomorrow night

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:34.200
to see where the story goes
and share with the public

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.834
and people who are watching
where there are surprises,

00:06:36.834 --> 00:06:38.234
where things are disappointing,

00:06:38.234 --> 00:06:40.000
where they're exciting,
where we won't know,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.767
and may not know in some cases
for a couple of days.

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:45.200
-Wow.
Before you leave, any --

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.300
as a person who communicates
for a living,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.000
any advice
on Thanksgiving dinner?

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.400
And what --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.534
How do you handle the --
-I mean, Jimmy,

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:55.534
everyone has, kind of, an Uncle
Frank or an Uncle Bob, right?

00:06:55.534 --> 00:06:58.234
I had an Uncle Bob.
There's people here.

00:06:58.234 --> 00:06:59.667
Uncle Bobs and Uncle Franks.
We'll give a shout-out to them.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:01.267
-Yeah, they know them. Yeah.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:03.634
-I would say, there's probably
going to be politics

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:06.367
at the dinner table
because Georgia is prob--

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:07.634
may go to a runoff.

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:09.701
We'll see what happens
tomorrow night.

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:11.567
And that means the election
in Georgia,

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:14.267
the runoff will be
in early December.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:15.868
And if that's the case,
you're going to have to --

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:16.834
everybody's going to
have to have

00:07:16.834 --> 00:07:18.133
these political conversations.

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:20.133
-My goodness.
-So, what I do -- I know.

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:21.901
What I do,
or how I think about it,

00:07:21.901 --> 00:07:23.734
is it's like a research project

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:25.834
or a research,
you know, process.

00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:27.534
But you don't
announce it in advance.

00:07:27.534 --> 00:07:31.133
So, if you are my -- Since I am
more on the progressive side,

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:33.367
if you were my conservative
Uncle Frank,

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:34.801
I would say, "Uncle Frank."
-Yeah?

00:07:34.801 --> 00:07:36.067
-I hope you're enjoying
your turkey.

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:37.100
-I love it.
-Great.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.000
Can you pass
the cranberry sauce?

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.434
Thank you.
-Sure, of course.

00:07:40.434 --> 00:07:42.167
-And as you're passing that
to me, I would say, "Frank --

00:07:42.167 --> 00:07:45.634
Uncle Frank, can you tell me
why you think there are chips

00:07:45.634 --> 00:07:48.801
engrained in people
making them vote Democratic?"

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:49.801
[ Laughter ]
"Tell me a little bit more

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:51.100
about what makes you think that.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.901
Oh, thank you
for the cranberry sauce.

00:07:52.901 --> 00:07:55.067
It's so good to see you."
-"Yeah. You're welcome. Yeah."

00:07:55.067 --> 00:07:57.434
-So, use it
as your own research process.

00:07:57.434 --> 00:07:59.000
Listen to people
who would disagree.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.434
-Do it while
they're in the middle

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:02.634
of passing some food?
-Don't announce it in advance,

00:08:02.634 --> 00:08:04.868
but that would be
my Thanksgiving advice.

00:08:04.868 --> 00:08:06.367
-There you go.
Jen Psaki, everybody.

00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:07.767
Thank you for that advice.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:07.767 --> 00:08:09.767
Break a leg tomorrow.
-Thank you.

00:08:09.767 --> 00:08:12.501
-Jen Psaki.
Watch her on MSNBC's election

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:15.334
night coverage tomorrow,
starting at 6:00 p.m.,

00:08:15.334 --> 00:08:19.200
then on NBC at 8:00 p.m.
Anything you need, we're here.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.567
I'm here for you, bud.
We'll be right back with

00:08:20.567 --> 00:08:22.801
a performance from Smino.
Stick around!

